1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:00,933
The Titanic.

2
00:00:00,934 --> 00:00:07,000
Watertight compartments in a steel-plated hull render it all but impenetrable.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Nearly every technological breakthrough of the previous 50 years is on board, providing comfort and safety.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
If it was built to be unsinkable, then why did it sink?

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Titanic Tech on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,740
The immensity of its floating hulk was too great to be contained in a single glance.

7
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,400
Surely it weighed hundreds of thousands of tons.

8
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,540
Much of its bulk rested beneath the surface of the calm seas.

9
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:56,480
It must have glistened even on a moonless April night on its maiden voyage.

10
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:04,640
Most marvelous of all, it was created entirely out of water, the iceberg that sank the Titanic.

11
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:16,220
Perhaps the story of the Titanic begins here, in the frigid North Atlantic, where icebergs are calved,

12
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:20,520
broken off from glaciers that drop into the ocean off Greenland's coast.

13
00:01:20,521 --> 00:01:29,340
It was here that the battle between technology and nature played out on that mid-April evening in 1912.

14
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,500
And it was here that technology lost.

15
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,900
How do you take the largest ship in the world on its maiden voyage,

16
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,020
full of the wealthiest, most influential people of two continents, you know,

17
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:46,560
that's called unsinkable, and crash it in the middle of the Atlantic before it ever completes its maiden voyage?

18
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:56,680
between 10 and 15 thousand icebergs form every year off greenland some nearly three million tons

19
00:01:56,681 --> 00:02:03,620
and containing ice as old as 110 thousand years and they continue to present a shipping hazard

20
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:10,180
they're deteriorating by the time they make it to the grand banks and eventually south out into the

21
00:02:10,181 --> 00:02:14,600
shipping lanes typically they're very dramatic in shape you know they've taken on irregular shapes

22
00:02:14,601 --> 00:02:17,020
and they're beautiful, but they're also very dangerous.

23
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:24,360
The International Ice Patrol, part of the United States Coast Guard,

24
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:30,780
monitors the ice using satellite images, radar, and C-130 reconnaissance planes.

25
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:39,320
Our job is to find the icebergs and then draw an imaginary line in the ocean

26
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:41,540
so that ships transiting across through there say,

27
00:02:41,541 --> 00:02:46,120
If you stay south of this line and east of this line, you're not going to encounter an iceberg like the Titanic did.

28
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:52,300
We tried to predict their drift and their deterioration because the mariner doesn't want to know where it was when we found it.

29
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:54,140
He wants to know where it's going to be when he gets there.

30
00:02:54,141 --> 00:02:59,000
So they don't violate that basic law of physics of trying to occupy the same space at the same time.

31
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:07,280
The ice patrol was created in the wake of the Titanic disaster and charged with monitoring ice in the North Atlantic shipping lanes.

32
00:03:07,281 --> 00:03:13,940
we're batting a thousand to use a baseball analogy since we've been in the business starting

33
00:03:13,941 --> 00:03:20,340
in 1913 even before our official ratification no one has hit an iceberg who has adhered to our

34
00:03:20,341 --> 00:03:25,120
limits ships still hit icebergs but they have been been inside the ice patrol's limits and

35
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:30,540
obviously there's never been a disaster that that comes anywhere close to the loss of life

36
00:03:30,541 --> 00:03:36,560
from the Titanic. Once a year on April 15th, the anniversary of their sinking,

37
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:43,980
the patrol tosses a wreath near the spot where the Titanic sank in honor of the roughly 1,500 lives

38
00:03:43,981 --> 00:03:44,681
lost.

39
00:03:44,682 --> 00:03:52,880
In stark contrast to the iceberg's simplicity was the White Star Line's Titanic,

40
00:03:52,881 --> 00:04:02,840
At the time, the biggest ship ever built at 882 and a half feet long, 92 and a half feet wide, and 53,000 tons.

41
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:12,240
From end to end, it was taller than the tallest skyscrapers in the world at that time.

42
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:21,340
Properly floating keel down, the ship was still as tall as an 11-story building.

43
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:28,380
It cruised the seas at 22 knots, or about 25 miles per hour.

44
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,120
Its builders considered it practically unsinkable,

45
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:38,220
because they incorporated into it expertise accumulated from a half century of building great passenger ships.

46
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:46,720
Most of the technology that existed in 1912 also existed 50 years prior,

47
00:04:46,721 --> 00:04:50,380
but it was in its infancy. It was in a development stage. It was just experimental.

48
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:54,000
The Titanic was a culmination of the development of all this stuff

49
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:56,020
and pulling it together and putting it into one package.

50
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:02,800
Titanic was actually the second of a class of three superliners

51
00:05:02,801 --> 00:05:09,840
that were projected to establish reliable big ship service between Europe and America.

52
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:18,280
The Olympic-class ships were really state-of-the-art, not cutting-edge, but state-of-the-art.

53
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:26,240
The ship was a manifestation of its opulent era, which began roughly a half century earlier,

54
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:29,600
in what became a golden age of invention and discovery.

55
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:36,900
Characterized by technological advances in the wake of the Industrial Revolution, the

56
00:05:36,901 --> 00:05:38,640
achievements of this golden age were many.

57
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:45,740
Thomas Newcomen's steam engine was improved and patented by James Watt in 1769.

58
00:05:45,741 --> 00:05:52,740
By the mid-19th century, gigantic coal-powered steam engines generated power for factories,

59
00:05:52,741 --> 00:05:57,740
propelled locomotives, and powered ships.

60
00:05:57,741 --> 00:06:01,740
The very coal that helped fuel the Industrial Revolution was now mined more productively

61
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:06,740
and efficiently because of the application of steam engines.

62
00:06:06,741 --> 00:06:10,740
They were used to pump out toxic gases and water from mines,

63
00:06:10,741 --> 00:06:14,740
allowing access to coal found in deeper, more hazardous veins.

64
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:28,453
Another technological advance of the mid-19th century was Henry Bessemer's ability to convert large amounts of iron into steel by forcing air through molten iron,

65
00:06:28,454 --> 00:06:33,240
which purged impurities, producing a lighter, stronger metal.

66
00:06:33,241 --> 00:06:43,240
Electricity generated by steam engines provided power for a host of other mid to late 19th

67
00:06:43,241 --> 00:06:51,900
century inventions, including the telephone, the elevator, the wireless telegraph, and

68
00:06:51,901 --> 00:06:53,138
the light bulb.

69
00:06:53,139 --> 00:06:59,660
This golden age lit a spotlight on how the natural world worked, and seemingly,

70
00:06:59,661 --> 00:07:04,380
it could be harnessed to do man's bidding.

71
00:07:04,381 --> 00:07:08,680
By the turn of the 20th century, shipbuilders also had what they needed to revolutionize

72
00:07:08,681 --> 00:07:10,720
ocean travel.

73
00:07:10,721 --> 00:07:14,160
And so began the era of the great passenger ships.

74
00:07:14,161 --> 00:07:20,060
England and Germany and France and Holland were your main maritime nations.

75
00:07:20,061 --> 00:07:25,780
The United States was a poor second cousin and they had the American line, but basically

76
00:07:25,781 --> 00:07:30,000
it was those four countries in Europe that had the large shipping.

77
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:36,560
England's two largest shipping lines, the Cunard and the White Star,

78
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:41,120
competed against each other, as well as against their competitors in other countries.

79
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:46,840
Their customers were primarily European immigrants, seeking a new life in the New World.

80
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:52,960
One thing that people don't understand is these ships were built for the immigrant trade,

81
00:07:52,961 --> 00:07:56,040
which was a flourishing type of economy in those days.

82
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:58,220
And there was only one way to cross the ocean.

83
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:02,000
So your ships were built for the immigrant trade,

84
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:07,040
and of course it was the only way that first and second class type of people could cross the ocean,

85
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:09,880
so they made the necessary accommodations for them.

86
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:16,040
The faster, more stylish Cunard Line trumped the White Star Line in 1907

87
00:08:16,041 --> 00:08:20,060
by introducing the two largest and fastest ships in the world,

88
00:08:20,061 --> 00:08:23,520
the Lusitania and the Mauritania.

89
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:28,720
Each was over 30,000 tons and reached speeds of 26 knots,

90
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:32,220
cutting transatlantic travel to as little as five days.

91
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:37,400
The White Star Line and its shipbuilders, Harland and Wolfe of Belfast, Ireland,

92
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:38,700
had to respond.

93
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:40,560
But how?

94
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:48,000
The conservative White Star Line had no intention of competing with Cunard over speed

95
00:08:48,001 --> 00:08:53,620
because the fuel costs of achieving certain speeds increases greatly with each additional knot.

96
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:58,680
Speed is very, very expensive.

97
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:04,460
And for, I think it is, for every knot over 21 knots,

98
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:08,360
you have to have the same amount of power to drive it to the next knot higher.

99
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:13,840
So coal consumption makes speed a very expensive operation.

100
00:09:13,841 --> 00:09:14,541
it.

101
00:09:14,542 --> 00:09:20,440
Instead, the White Star Line and Harland and Wolfe responded by trying to create the

102
00:09:20,441 --> 00:09:26,997
largest, most luxurious ships ever built. Olympic, Titanic, and Britannic.

103
00:09:26,998 --> 00:09:28,060
If they were

104
00:09:28,061 --> 00:09:34,320
successful, the Olympic-class ships would answer Cunard's speed with sheer, splendiferous

105
00:09:34,321 --> 00:09:35,021
bulk.

106
00:09:35,022 --> 00:09:43,460
Tradition dictated that the names of White Star ships end in IC,

107
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:47,480
as in Olympic, Titanic, and Britannic,

108
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:50,620
just as the names of Cunard ships end in IA,

109
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:53,280
as in Mauritania and Lusitania.

110
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,840
Titanic Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

111
00:09:56,841 --> 00:10:10,120
construction of the titanic began on march 31st 1909 three months after her sister ship the olympic

112
00:10:10,121 --> 00:10:18,140
here in belfast northern ireland the shipyard for lord paris harland and wolf on the irish sea

113
00:10:18,141 --> 00:10:26,040
Harlan and Wolfe gained a reputation for being able to build large ships.

114
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:33,020
They concentrated on two areas, large ship technology and steam engine technology.

115
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:39,840
So when Lord Perry and Ismay got together and designed the Olympic-class ships,

116
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:47,120
the Harlan and Wolfe shipyard was in a good position to accommodate the building of these two hulls.

117
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:53,300
But the blueprints called for ships so large that the yard had to be redesigned to accommodate them.

118
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:59,340
The shipyard had to radically change a lot of stuff in order to build those two ships.

119
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:02,860
They had to take a space which was normally used for building three ships

120
00:11:02,861 --> 00:11:06,260
and reduce it to two building slips and put up a tremendous gantry over it

121
00:11:06,261 --> 00:11:09,080
to handle the heavy weights that were going to be moved along underneath.

122
00:11:09,081 --> 00:11:13,400
About four and a half feet of concrete had to be sunk into the slipways of the base

123
00:11:13,401 --> 00:11:18,820
to make two giant slipways to erect these two holes.

124
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:21,340
A large gantry was designed.

125
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:27,140
This gantry would be used to suspend things like the hydraulic riveter, various other tools.

126
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:30,800
It uses it as a crane to lift heavy objects up to the heights they needed.

127
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:39,280
The 220-foot-high, 6,000-ton gantry crane was the largest of its kind in the world at that time.

128
00:11:39,281 --> 00:11:46,280
Harland and Wolfe's Thomas Andrews was in charge of implementing the design of the ships,

129
00:11:46,281 --> 00:11:51,280
closely scrutinized by managing director Lord Parry, who developed the requirements.

130
00:11:51,281 --> 00:11:57,550
The White Star Line also monitored each stage of the planning and construction through its director, J.

131
00:11:57,551 --> 00:11:58,280
Bruce Ismay.

132
00:11:58,281 --> 00:12:02,280
Harland and Wolfe was the largest single employer in Belfast,

133
00:12:02,281 --> 00:12:08,280
and more than 15,000 workers labored six days a week to construct the massive ships.

134
00:12:08,281 --> 00:12:11,300
And though it must have been dramatic to see these behemoths take shape,

135
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:14,520
there was little about the design of the ships that was unusual.

136
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:21,120
The single technological flourish in the Titanic was size.

137
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:25,120
I think she was evolutionary, but she was not revolutionary.

138
00:12:25,121 --> 00:12:28,120
Her main distinction still remained size.

139
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:32,360
Harlan and Wolfe was the tried and true.

140
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:34,400
They'd done it for decades.

141
00:12:34,401 --> 00:12:38,500
They just built basically the same ships over and over again.

142
00:12:40,280 --> 00:12:45,400
The process of building the Titanic, or any other ship, begins with laying down the keel,

143
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:47,860
which runs along the bottom of the ship's center.

144
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:52,400
A steel ship is built using a skeletal structure.

145
00:12:53,140 --> 00:12:58,700
You can think of the keel as a spine, and then ribs that come up from the spine,

146
00:12:59,180 --> 00:13:02,340
and then a skin over that to make a complete structure.

147
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:17,420
The Olympic and the Titanic each had a double bottom, or watertight space between the keel and the lowest deck of the ship.

148
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:24,240
This common feature was primarily for safety, and secondarily a storage area for water ballast.

149
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:32,120
Double hulling the ships, a process that added an extra hull of steel outside the existing hull, would have increased their safety,

150
00:13:32,121 --> 00:13:35,560
but would have been both expensive and unusual for the time.

151
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:40,680
Then ribs went vertically up.

152
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:46,680
Three feet spacing was the average between the ribs, the length of the vessel.

153
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:52,840
As the ribs began to take shape, then you started to put in deck beams

154
00:13:52,841 --> 00:13:58,240
to give it longitudinal rigidity as you're building it up.

155
00:13:58,241 --> 00:14:09,340
Once the framing process is done, and the framing process took about a year, then you began the plating.

156
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:21,140
The nearly 26,000 ton hull would have a skin of overlapping steel plates, each one inch thick and as large as 30 by 6 feet.

157
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:27,520
The steel was forged using the open hearth process, an improvement over the older Bessemer process.

158
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:33,800
The method called for large brick ovens that reach temperatures of nearly 3,000 degrees.

159
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:43,160
This process of making the steel on an open hearth was a bit more efficient

160
00:14:43,161 --> 00:14:48,440
because you could melt scrap iron plus ore together

161
00:14:48,441 --> 00:14:55,460
and reuse some byproducts from other processes and generate the steel that way.

162
00:14:55,461 --> 00:14:58,900
That was the state-of-the-art technology at the time.

163
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:03,800
It was about the best and most efficient way to produce mild steel.

164
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:11,260
Steel on the Olympic, Titanic, and many other ships of the day was prone to micro-cracking,

165
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,360
a condition causing minuscule fissures, particularly around rivet holes.

166
00:15:18,040 --> 00:15:21,680
Harland and Wolfe was aware of this, but considered it to be a manageable condition.

167
00:15:21,681 --> 00:15:30,200
steel plates will be fastened over the entire hull by more than three million rivets each

168
00:15:30,201 --> 00:15:37,120
three inches long the rivets that were hydraulically driven into the ship they

169
00:15:37,121 --> 00:15:46,280
had these huge hydraulic presses that they hung from the gantry that that was suspended over the

170
00:15:46,281 --> 00:15:48,980
holes as they were building in Belfast.

171
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:53,960
Hydraulic riveting was used on a great part of the ship,

172
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,180
but when the machine couldn't fit, it was all done by hand.

173
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:02,920
Although state-of-the-art at the time, the method was not flawless.

174
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:08,800
Imperfect metal called slag sometimes found its way into the wrought iron rivets,

175
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:10,480
which were hammered by hand.

176
00:16:10,481 --> 00:16:24,481
Slag in the wrought iron rivets is the result of the fact that neither the Bessemer nor the open hearth process of making the steel were perfect in extracting all its

177
00:16:26,949 --> 00:16:28,040
impurities.

178
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:36,460
Some of it eventually finds its way into the making of the rivet, and that slag will weaken the rivet.

179
00:16:36,461 --> 00:16:45,600
Although these rivets would not pass today's standards, they were the highest quality achievable at the time and were considered more than adequate.

180
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:54,700
Inside the hull were 15 bulkheads separating 16 watertight compartments lined up in the bottom of the ship.

181
00:16:56,120 --> 00:17:00,140
Virtually all ships like the Titanic would have had similar watertight bulkheads.

182
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:05,900
Even with the four front compartments breached, or two of the side compartments breached,

183
00:17:06,220 --> 00:17:10,700
the ship would not sink, and a collision breaching more than one compartment was rare.

184
00:17:13,580 --> 00:17:19,520
However, bulkhead walls were not sealed at the top, because the designers considered it unnecessary.

185
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:23,280
Based on the number of breached compartments the ship could survive,

186
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,500
water levels inside the compartments should never have risen over the walls.

187
00:17:27,501 --> 00:17:34,380
If water did flood over the walls, it would indicate more than a survivable number of compartments were breached,

188
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:39,080
and the ship was experiencing a fatal loss of buoyancy, causing it to sink,

189
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:42,640
whether water flowed over the compartment walls or not.

190
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:51,940
Harlan and Wolf was very sensible and practical about their application of what they had learned from experience

191
00:17:51,941 --> 00:17:53,740
and what we're going to use in our ships.

192
00:17:54,500 --> 00:18:01,140
They went for safety, comfort, ironically safety, with all the water type subdivision and so forth.

193
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:12,180
On May 31, 1911, the Titanic's hull was launched from the building gantry nearly seven months after the Olympics hull was launched in 1910.

194
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:19,060
Over 100,000 people watched from the yard as the ship slid down the slip into the River Lagan,

195
00:18:19,061 --> 00:18:25,560
with the aid of more than 22 tons of grease, consisting of fat, soap, and train oil.

196
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:30,880
A workman was killed just prior to the launch, the second death attributed to construction.

197
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:38,320
He unfortunately was removing the poles or the lumber underneath and one fell on him.

198
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,100
And he later died in a Belfast hospital.

199
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:46,200
Lost lives were simply the cost of doing business,

200
00:18:46,201 --> 00:18:50,460
with one loss expected for roughly every 100,000 tons of construction.

201
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:55,600
In addition to the human toll was the financial one.

202
00:18:57,380 --> 00:19:01,640
The Titanic would ultimately cost Harland and Wolf $7.5 million,

203
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:05,320
equal to well over $120 million today.

204
00:19:06,540 --> 00:19:09,720
But at this stage, the ship was still merely an empty hull.

205
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:16,020
Three large anchors, three massive propellers,

206
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:19,340
and four funnels were designed and built for the ship.

207
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:24,440
The anchors were forged and hammered of rough cast steel.

208
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:28,780
The side anchors were 7 3⁄4 tons each.

209
00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:31,660
The center anchor was 15 tons,

210
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:34,920
and there's a famous picture of all the horses carrying it to the yard.

211
00:19:36,780 --> 00:19:41,060
Two three-bladed side propellers, each 23 1⁄2 feet across,

212
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:43,020
weighed in at 38 tons apiece.

213
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,960
A four-bladed center propeller was 16 1⁄2 feet across and weighed 22 tons.

214
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:53,100
The propellers were made at Harlan and Wolf's Yard themselves.

215
00:19:53,220 --> 00:19:56,000
They did have a foundry at that time that produced certain parts.

216
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:59,680
The Castile bosses for the wing screws and the bronze blades were both produced there,

217
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:02,980
as well as the four-bladed center screw, which was also manganese bronze.

218
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:07,440
Four oval funnels lined the center of the ship.

219
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:13,980
Three of the four funnels were necessary for ventilation of combustion and flue gases from the boiler rooms.

220
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:19,700
The fourth one was not necessary for removing boiler gases, but it was added for aesthetics partially,

221
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:22,380
but also was used as a ventilator for the turbine engine rooms,

222
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:25,240
and it also had a pipe that came up from the inside from the galleys,

223
00:20:25,300 --> 00:20:27,500
which vented off the coal smoke from those stoves and ovens.

224
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:32,080
More than 2,000 portholes and windows studded the ship.

225
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:37,960
Two 205-foot masts were attached, one with a derrick for lifting motor cars,

226
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:44,300
and a ladder inside for climbing to the crow's nest to watch for, among other things, icebergs.

227
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:49,380
It would take nearly a year to complete the fitting out of the Titanic.

228
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,960
Fitting out, basically that's the completing of the ship.

229
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:58,780
You take an empty shell and you turn it into a palatial floating palace.

230
00:20:58,781 --> 00:21:04,840
Harland and Wolfe now began to incorporate not just the fixtures and fabrics and woodwork,

231
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:09,760
but the technology that would make the Titanic a catalog of Edwardian achievement.

232
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:17,640
The Titanic and the Olympic were so large that the White Star piers in New York City

233
00:21:17,641 --> 00:21:22,000
had to be extended by 100 feet so that the ships could be berthed in the harbor.

234
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:25,280
Titanic Tech will return on Modern Marvelous.

235
00:21:28,780 --> 00:21:37,640
Although the Titanic was the largest ship in the world, all three of the Olympic-class ships were designed to be 882 1⁄2 feet long.

236
00:21:38,720 --> 00:21:42,620
Olympic and Titanic were built from the same basic drawings exactly.

237
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:45,680
They're exactly the same length, the same width, the same height.

238
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:48,940
They were designed originally to be identical twins.

239
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:56,440
But modifications to the Titanic increased her weight to 53,000 tons, thus making her the largest.

240
00:21:56,441 --> 00:21:59,600
and she was just as imposing inside as out.

241
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:04,300
The heart of all power on the Titanic came from steam.

242
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:07,000
The generation of steam propelled the ship.

243
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:11,440
It lit the ship, made electrical power for all the stuff on board.

244
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:13,720
It ran the refrigeration systems. It ran everything.

245
00:22:14,680 --> 00:22:18,440
There were six boiler rooms occupying a space of about 320 feet

246
00:22:18,441 --> 00:22:19,640
throughout the length of the ship.

247
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:24,760
29 15-foot-high boilers were installed in the hull.

248
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:30,600
Next to the boilers were the ship's engines, two reciprocating engines and a steam turbine.

249
00:22:32,380 --> 00:22:35,920
The two engines turned the side propellers, or wing screws.

250
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:45,404
Each of these reciprocating engines had a high-pressure cylinder, an intermediate pressure cylinder, and a low-pressure cylinder, each one driving a crank on that

251
00:22:45,405 --> 00:22:46,105
crankshaft.

252
00:22:46,106 --> 00:22:55,000
steam was admitted at approximately 210 pounds per square inch to the high pressure cylinder

253
00:22:55,001 --> 00:22:59,760
it was exhausted from the high pressure cylinders to the intermediate at 78 pounds per square inch

254
00:22:59,761 --> 00:23:06,140
and then from the intermediate to the low pressure at 24 pounds per square inch

255
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:12,080
after the steam exited the low pressure cylinder it was still useful within the vacuum of the

256
00:23:12,081 --> 00:23:12,854
closed pipes.

257
00:23:12,855 --> 00:23:17,680
In Titanic's case, they took that remaining steam, which exited out of the engine

258
00:23:17,681 --> 00:23:26,320
at 9 psi, and they diverted it into a low-pressure turbine, which used the steam to impact against

259
00:23:26,321 --> 00:23:33,256
thousands of blades attached to rotors. The turbine turned the center screw.

260
00:23:33,257 --> 00:23:34,260
After that,

261
00:23:34,420 --> 00:23:39,980
whatever steam was left was diverted to condensers, turned back into water, and fed back into the

262
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:43,968
boilers in a closed system. It was extremely efficient.

263
00:23:43,969 --> 00:23:46,360
Ships only 50 years before it did

264
00:23:46,361 --> 00:23:50,236
not have this sort of stuff and they burned tremendous amounts of fuel.

265
00:23:50,237 --> 00:23:51,000
The ship could

266
00:23:51,001 --> 00:23:56,029
carry nearly 6,000 tons of coal in its massive bunkers.

267
00:23:56,030 --> 00:23:59,320
Using approximately 625 tons of coal

268
00:23:59,321 --> 00:24:05,260
a day at a speed of 22 knots, the Titanic's engines produced in the range of 46,000 to

269
00:24:05,261 --> 00:24:11,320
50,000 horsepower. But that's not all they did. They also generated electricity.

270
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:16,856
Titanic's electrical plant consisted of four generating sets.

271
00:24:16,857 --> 00:24:19,060
Each generating set consisted

272
00:24:19,061 --> 00:24:26,540
of an engine coupled to a 400 kilowatt dynamo, which generated 100 volt direct current that was

273
00:24:26,541 --> 00:24:28,147
used in the ship.

274
00:24:28,148 --> 00:24:35,140
It was used for a number of things, but primarily electricity was used for

275
00:24:35,141 --> 00:24:42,040
ventilation, to run all the fans, for heating, for the electrical heaters throughout the

276
00:24:42,041 --> 00:24:44,460
ship, and also for illumination.

277
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:51,240
Electricity was relatively new to passenger ships, and the Titanic indulged in it like

278
00:24:51,241 --> 00:24:57,080
no other ship before, with over 200 miles of electric cable and more than 10,000 light

279
00:24:57,081 --> 00:24:59,720
bulbs, illuminating the ship almost to excess.

280
00:24:59,721 --> 00:25:05,640
Electricity from the engine also ran one of the three closed telephone systems on board.

281
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:10,880
One with a 50-phone switchboard and another that connected the crow's nest to the bridge.

282
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:15,680
Electricity ran equipment in the first-class gymnasium,

283
00:25:16,580 --> 00:25:18,580
powered heaters in the first-class rooms,

284
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:21,640
even the Turkish baths on F-deck.

285
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:25,160
There were Turkish baths on other ships,

286
00:25:25,161 --> 00:25:28,920
but they just pulled out all the stops here for Olympic and Titanic

287
00:25:28,921 --> 00:25:33,720
with a very elaborate Arabian-style cooling room.

288
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:36,480
They had a shampooing room, hot room, steam room.

289
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:41,600
Another relative novelty was the four sets of elevators,

290
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:45,700
three for first class and, in an unusually inclusive gesture,

291
00:25:46,480 --> 00:25:47,860
one for second class.

292
00:25:49,100 --> 00:25:50,820
Third class took the stairs.

293
00:25:53,480 --> 00:25:56,560
Select first-class suites had their own private baths,

294
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:58,140
an extreme luxury at the time.

295
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:04,040
But all 710 passengers and steerage shared just two bathtubs.

296
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:09,580
But on such a short trip, most passengers wouldn't miss their weekly bath if they waited until the end of the voyage.

297
00:26:11,580 --> 00:26:18,460
Three water systems supply drinking water, distilled water for hand and dishwashing, and seawater for baths and toilets.

298
00:26:23,220 --> 00:26:26,580
Small tanks also provided water to sinks in some rooms.

299
00:26:28,920 --> 00:26:35,920
First-class passengers could also avail themselves of a 30-foot-long, 14-foot-wide swimming pool,

300
00:26:35,921 --> 00:26:40,920
a luxury previously seen on only one other ship before the Olympic and the Titanic,

301
00:26:40,921 --> 00:26:43,920
but on a much smaller scale.

302
00:26:43,921 --> 00:26:49,920
On September 20, 1911, Titanic's efficient construction schedule was disrupted.

303
00:26:49,921 --> 00:26:55,920
As the Titanic was still being fitted out, the Olympic collided with another ship.

304
00:26:55,921 --> 00:27:01,720
Attention was diverted from the Titanic as workmen labored to repair the gaping hole in the Olympics hull.

305
00:27:02,760 --> 00:27:08,960
Some argued that the enormous size of the ships in comparison to their rudders contributed to a lack of maneuverability.

306
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:14,060
Five stories tall and weighing 101 tons, the rudder was hardly small.

307
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:16,700
But was it big enough for such a massive ship?

308
00:27:18,420 --> 00:27:20,880
Much has been made about the rudder being too small.

309
00:27:21,740 --> 00:27:23,100
This is another one of these myths.

310
00:27:23,101 --> 00:27:27,040
based on data that still exists from the Olympics trials.

311
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:30,500
The turning circles and the amount of time it took to change that ship's direction

312
00:27:30,501 --> 00:27:32,600
are perfectly acceptable within today's standards.

313
00:27:34,660 --> 00:27:37,920
Unconcerned that Olympics collision might be the result of a design flaw,

314
00:27:38,620 --> 00:27:41,140
Harland and Wolfe conducted the Titanic sea trials,

315
00:27:41,440 --> 00:27:43,640
a period during which the ship's systems are tested,

316
00:27:44,540 --> 00:27:48,840
on April 2, 1912, putting the ship through turns and maneuvers.

317
00:27:48,841 --> 00:27:57,840
Its satisfied owners had no reason to postpone the Titanic's maiden voyage, set for eight days later, April 10, 1912.

318
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:06,840
Cargo on the Titanic included a new Renault automobile, 300 cases of walnuts, two barrels of mercury,

319
00:28:07,840 --> 00:28:13,840
and a single rare jewel-encrusted copy of the literary classic, The Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam.

320
00:28:13,841 --> 00:28:20,620
Titanic Tech will return on Modern Marvel.

321
00:28:26,480 --> 00:28:31,620
The Titanic set off from Southampton for Cherbourg on April 10, 1912,

322
00:28:31,621 --> 00:28:34,060
with roughly 1,300 passengers,

323
00:28:35,220 --> 00:28:37,720
including the Titanic's designer, Thomas Andrews,

324
00:28:37,960 --> 00:28:41,140
and White Star Line president, J. Bruce Ismay.

325
00:28:41,141 --> 00:28:49,140
Edward John Smith, a veteran White Star ship's captain, may have considered making this maiden voyage as his last before retiring.

326
00:28:51,780 --> 00:28:57,360
He joined a nearly 900-person crew that ushered passengers onto their respective decks.

327
00:28:58,620 --> 00:29:03,020
The luxury was evident immediately, at least to first- and second-class passengers.

328
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:08,280
A first-class lounge was even patterned after the palace at Versailles.

329
00:29:08,281 --> 00:29:13,560
But the real architectural flourish of the ship was a five-story wooden staircase.

330
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:23,380
To my mind, the grand staircase of Titanic was by far her most compelling and attractive architectural feature.

331
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:28,940
I've never seen anything quite like it before or since in any other ship.

332
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:31,620
It just looks perfect to me.

333
00:29:31,720 --> 00:29:33,680
The dome overhead is just perfect.

334
00:29:33,760 --> 00:29:35,460
Not too much, not too little.

335
00:29:35,461 --> 00:29:42,460
Still, it wasn't unique. Other ships had multi-deck staircases.

336
00:29:42,461 --> 00:29:48,460
Though the Titanic's elegance was undeniable, it was also old-fashioned.

337
00:29:48,461 --> 00:29:52,460
Harland and Wolf's carpenters built the same style furniture,

338
00:29:52,461 --> 00:29:58,460
laid the same patterned carpeting, and installed the same stained glass windows as they had in previous ships.

339
00:29:58,461 --> 00:30:02,460
Stylistically, they tried little that was new.

340
00:30:02,461 --> 00:30:07,180
They were somewhat pedestrian, oddly enough.

341
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:10,520
I've never understood why they didn't try.

342
00:30:10,660 --> 00:30:15,740
They had the opportunity with Olympic and Titanic to do something very different.

343
00:30:16,220 --> 00:30:19,380
And they went for the very same patterns, the very same fabrics,

344
00:30:19,381 --> 00:30:21,920
the very same carvings in some of the public rooms

345
00:30:21,921 --> 00:30:24,940
that could be seen in some of their earlier ships.

346
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:30,060
If the Titanic wasn't exactly stylistically up to date,

347
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:31,980
it was safety conscious.

348
00:30:32,460 --> 00:30:36,000
In addition to the double bottom and the watertight bulkheads,

349
00:30:36,740 --> 00:30:40,700
watertight doors in those bulkheads could be locked and sealed electrically from the bridge.

350
00:30:42,220 --> 00:30:45,540
Although the original plans called for 64 lifeboats,

351
00:30:45,920 --> 00:30:50,660
the final tally of 20 exceeded the legal limit for British-flagged ships of her size.

352
00:30:52,720 --> 00:30:55,540
It's amazing how people talk about lifeboats.

353
00:30:56,280 --> 00:30:59,480
There weren't enough on the Titanic and this type of thing.

354
00:30:59,481 --> 00:31:05,760
But one must remember all steamship companies of the time had similar faults.

355
00:31:06,560 --> 00:31:12,740
Lifeboat accommodation was based on tonnage, not the number of people you carried.

356
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:20,500
And in fact, the Titanic had more lifeboats than what the requirement called for.

357
00:31:20,840 --> 00:31:26,560
She had 14 wooden boats, she had two emergency cutters, and she had four collapsible boats.

358
00:31:26,561 --> 00:31:35,800
With room for 1,200 passengers in the lifeboats, mariners considered this more than sufficient for a ship with a capacity of over 3,000.

359
00:31:38,360 --> 00:31:46,460
Lifeboats were meant only to ferry passengers from a damaged or disabled ship, not to sustain every passenger on board indefinitely on the open seas.

360
00:31:48,740 --> 00:31:54,480
And why should Harland and Wolf worry now that science had provided the wireless for ships to communicate at sea?

361
00:31:55,480 --> 00:31:59,560
This was arguably the single greatest technological achievement on the Titanic,

362
00:31:59,780 --> 00:32:02,620
and possibly its most significant safety device.

363
00:32:03,500 --> 00:32:06,040
And it was unlike that on any other ship.

364
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:12,140
She was the only one afloat that carried a Marconi rotary disc.

365
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:15,200
Titanic had 16 studs around the periphery of her disc.

366
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:17,460
This thing spun at a high rate of speed,

367
00:32:18,120 --> 00:32:23,620
and every time the electrodes lined up, the spark jumped between them.

368
00:32:23,621 --> 00:32:30,020
This caused more hits and gave Titanic's spark a very musical tone.

369
00:32:30,720 --> 00:32:37,360
The spark discharged across the electrodes 120 times per second, which resulted in a clear high-density tone.

370
00:32:38,020 --> 00:32:42,960
When the operator pressed the key, a circuit was completed, allowing current to flow through the transmitter.

371
00:32:44,200 --> 00:32:48,340
Opening and closing the circuit created the Morse code characters comprising each message.

372
00:32:48,341 --> 00:32:55,580
And it was the tone itself that carried farther, not the brute power of the spark, but the actual tone.

373
00:32:56,720 --> 00:33:04,220
The Titanic's Marconi wireless had a range of 250 to 400 miles during the day, and even farther at night.

374
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:13,040
They were testing the wireless in Belfast, and the wireless operators actually picked up Cairo.

375
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:16,620
So that was quite a distance back then.

376
00:33:18,340 --> 00:33:21,440
In the unlikely event that the Titanic was damaged,

377
00:33:21,441 --> 00:33:25,280
a signal could be sent to nearby ships to aid in rescue operations.

378
00:33:26,600 --> 00:33:30,440
Ships could also warn each other about potential trouble, such as icebergs.

379
00:33:33,240 --> 00:33:35,560
But despite this emphasis on safety,

380
00:33:35,840 --> 00:33:38,880
trouble plagued the Titanic before it ever left Southampton Harbor.

381
00:33:39,980 --> 00:33:44,980
Its massive hull displaced so much water that a nearby ship nearly collided with it.

382
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:52,220
Also, a persistent coal fire buried in a bunker smoldered for several days.

383
00:33:53,880 --> 00:33:58,400
You would have spontaneous combustion in areas of coal bunkers.

384
00:33:58,860 --> 00:34:03,420
It happened quite often. It was not raging flames or anything like that.

385
00:34:04,040 --> 00:34:08,220
But they would just continue drawing the coal until the seat of the fire was revealed,

386
00:34:08,221 --> 00:34:10,280
and then they would extinguish the embers.

387
00:34:10,281 --> 00:34:20,120
The Titanic made two otherwise uneventful stops before heading for America, with just over 2,200 passengers and crew.

388
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:26,640
Wireless messages of congratulations were mixed with warnings of ice in the sea lanes.

389
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:32,920
As these were sent to the bridge, Captain Smith ordered the ship into a more southerly route.

390
00:34:32,921 --> 00:34:41,940
A six-hour wireless breakdown on the ship added to a backlog of messages, including iceberg warnings over the weekend.

391
00:34:44,140 --> 00:34:53,480
By Sunday, April 14th, with the wireless finally repaired and the coal fire out, the ship was traveling at nearly full power, 22 knots.

392
00:34:54,880 --> 00:34:58,660
It was a moonless night, and the seas were remarkably calm.

393
00:34:59,320 --> 00:35:03,600
Calm dark seas may be good for passengers, but bad for spotting icebergs,

394
00:35:03,880 --> 00:35:07,660
since no waves would lap against the ice and no moon would reflect off it.

395
00:35:07,960 --> 00:35:13,080
But at approximately 11.40 p.m., a warning rang out from the crow's nest.

396
00:35:13,820 --> 00:35:15,180
An iceberg was dead ahead.

397
00:35:15,740 --> 00:35:22,560
As the Titanic bore down upon it, no liner ever built was better prepared to withstand such a collision.

398
00:35:23,940 --> 00:35:27,560
Originally, the Titanic's newer sister ship was to be called the Gigantic,

399
00:35:27,561 --> 00:35:36,300
But after the Titanic sank, the hull, which was already under construction, was renamed Britannic to avoid the appearance of arrogance.

400
00:35:37,440 --> 00:35:39,860
Titanic Tech will return on Modern Marvelous.

401
00:35:44,660 --> 00:35:48,800
As the Titanic bore down on an iceberg directly in its path,

402
00:35:49,460 --> 00:35:57,340
later estimates determined that 37 seconds elapsed from the moment the crow's nest alerted the bridge until the Titanic collided with it.

403
00:35:57,560 --> 00:36:03,100
I corresponded with Frederick Fleet, who was in the crow's nest of the Titanic,

404
00:36:03,460 --> 00:36:07,900
and he said when he first saw the iceberg, it looked like just a little bump on the horizon.

405
00:36:08,620 --> 00:36:13,240
But he immediately picked up the telephone that was in the crow's nest

406
00:36:13,241 --> 00:36:17,420
and called the bridge and said, iceberg right ahead, sir.

407
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:23,440
Berg was approximately one mile away with the ship traveling at roughly 21 knots.

408
00:36:24,100 --> 00:36:27,140
First Officer Murdoch ordered the wheel turned and engines reversed.

409
00:36:27,560 --> 00:36:33,240
to maneuver out of the way but there simply wasn't time or room for the titanic to clear the berg

410
00:36:33,241 --> 00:36:35,800
instead murdoch sealed the ship's fate

411
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:49,240
if the ship hadn't been going you know a fraction of a knot that fast if it had been going any slower

412
00:36:49,241 --> 00:36:54,040
the bow would have you know missed it it would have it was starting to turn it almost missed

413
00:36:54,041 --> 00:36:58,880
the berg, if it had just had five seconds more warning or five seconds later warning,

414
00:36:58,960 --> 00:37:00,918
it might have hit the berg more direct.

415
00:37:00,919 --> 00:37:03,680
The ship was designed to withstand a head-on collision.

416
00:37:03,681 --> 00:37:09,320
It was that glancing scrape all along the side that was the death knell.

417
00:37:10,300 --> 00:37:14,960
The collision likely caused the rivets on the steel plates of the hull below water level

418
00:37:14,961 --> 00:37:20,140
to pop under pressure along a 220-foot line, breaching six compartments.

419
00:37:20,141 --> 00:37:24,400
all Murdoch knew was that they'd hit.

420
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:28,300
He went to the lever to close the watertight doors.

421
00:37:30,700 --> 00:37:33,780
And from that point on, Captain Smith came up on the bridge

422
00:37:33,781 --> 00:37:36,700
and this was just after they struck the berg

423
00:37:36,701 --> 00:37:39,000
and Captain Smith said, what happened?

424
00:37:39,080 --> 00:37:41,500
And Murdoch said, we've struck an iceberg, sir.

425
00:37:41,660 --> 00:37:44,280
I tried hard to pour it around her, but she was too close.

426
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:48,980
And from that point on, the Titanic, of course, was mortally wounded.

427
00:37:50,140 --> 00:37:59,340
There is also evidence to suggest that the ship may have temporarily ridden up onto the iceberg instead of, or in addition to, grading it along the side.

428
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:06,700
A survivor claimed he saw water rushing into a portion of the hull that could not have been breached.

429
00:38:08,640 --> 00:38:12,320
Fireman Hendrickson saw water pouring into the fireman's passage.

430
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:15,660
That passage was compromised somehow.

431
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:17,640
It was open to the sea.

432
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:19,800
It was a large amount of water.

433
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:23,500
yet the fireman's passage was in the center of the ship.

434
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:26,180
It was three feet away from the skin of the ship.

435
00:38:26,760 --> 00:38:32,360
Now, under no account do we speculate that the iceberg penetrated three feet into the side of the ship.

436
00:38:32,960 --> 00:38:38,520
However, the fireman's passage was supported on stanchions coming from the bottom of the ship.

437
00:38:39,440 --> 00:38:41,640
If this eyewitness account is accurate,

438
00:38:41,641 --> 00:38:45,160
water must have been pouring into a watertight compartment from below.

439
00:38:45,161 --> 00:38:51,700
designer Thomas Andrews joined a carpenter sent by Captain Smith to sound or inspect the ship

440
00:38:51,701 --> 00:38:59,320
what they saw confirmed their worst fears Andrews calculated that the ship was doomed

441
00:38:59,321 --> 00:39:04,360
as soon as he saw the extent of the damage and I have to wonder what Andrew saw during

442
00:39:04,361 --> 00:39:10,480
his inspection I would like to know if he knew that there were other points of ingress for the

443
00:39:10,481 --> 00:39:19,160
water and based on that he was able to calculate that at a certain point in this sinking process,

444
00:39:19,660 --> 00:39:25,280
the water was going to find these non-water type decks and once it did that, the ship was doomed.

445
00:39:27,100 --> 00:39:31,420
Despite theories about the micro cracking of the ship's steel and weakened rivets,

446
00:39:31,421 --> 00:39:35,940
there was no conclusive evidence that either played a significant part in causing the disaster.

447
00:39:36,940 --> 00:39:40,680
The same is true for the theory that the coal fire may have weakened the hull.

448
00:39:42,560 --> 00:39:46,200
Simply put, the collision alone was catastrophic enough.

449
00:39:48,220 --> 00:39:52,960
A stunned Captain Smith eventually gave the order to lower the lifeboats just after midnight.

450
00:39:54,320 --> 00:39:58,040
But at this early stage, people didn't really believe the ship would sink.

451
00:39:59,160 --> 00:40:04,640
Some lifeboats had a 65-person capacity, but boats were launched with as few as 12 people.

452
00:40:05,940 --> 00:40:08,900
Meanwhile, the Marconi operators began sending distress messages.

453
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:14,260
However, no ship was close enough to respond before the Titanic would have sunk.

454
00:40:16,060 --> 00:40:20,160
As Titanic was sinking and sending out her distress calls,

455
00:40:20,161 --> 00:40:25,160
you had this 60 hertz of musical tone

456
00:40:25,161 --> 00:40:31,860
carrying above all these whispers from all these other plane spark discharges.

457
00:40:35,940 --> 00:40:39,640
You've got this musical tone that's very clear and concise.

458
00:40:40,260 --> 00:40:41,620
There's no doubt it was Titanic.

459
00:40:44,500 --> 00:40:47,820
And this message carried Titanic's death row.

460
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:54,120
Within three hours, the ship's loss of buoyancy pulled it down,

461
00:40:54,820 --> 00:40:57,540
splitting it in two between the third and fourth funnels.

462
00:41:00,740 --> 00:41:04,700
The ship deformed from the bottom up,

463
00:41:04,701 --> 00:41:08,860
and eventually as it separated, then it tore the steel plating,

464
00:41:09,220 --> 00:41:12,220
it tore the superstructure apart, and the two sections separated.

465
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:20,800
The keel was very strong and evidently kept the bow and stern sections connected

466
00:41:20,801 --> 00:41:23,200
for a short while after the breakup.

467
00:41:23,940 --> 00:41:27,040
The bow section by this time was almost completely flooded

468
00:41:27,041 --> 00:41:28,740
and began its descent to the bottom.

469
00:41:28,741 --> 00:41:34,760
The rest of the story is one of human tragedy and not technology.

470
00:41:36,600 --> 00:41:39,560
Just over 700 people made it to the lifeboats.

471
00:41:41,080 --> 00:41:43,780
More than 1,500 did not.

472
00:41:44,600 --> 00:41:49,260
Within half an hour of the ship sinking, hypothermia had silenced their shrieks.

473
00:41:50,960 --> 00:41:55,180
The Cunard ship Carpathia was the first ship to respond to the distress call,

474
00:41:55,181 --> 00:41:59,200
arriving the following morning and plucking survivors from the boats,

475
00:42:00,740 --> 00:42:02,860
including the White Star's J. Bruce Ismay.

476
00:42:03,920 --> 00:42:09,860
Back in New York, a congressional investigation began almost before the survivors disembarked.

477
00:42:12,380 --> 00:42:15,020
Stories traveled across the nation and the world,

478
00:42:15,960 --> 00:42:18,820
using the modern wire services syndicated in newspapers.

479
00:42:19,520 --> 00:42:21,260
Through the application of this technology,

480
00:42:21,261 --> 00:42:25,360
the Titanic tragedy became arguably the world's first media spectacle.

481
00:42:26,420 --> 00:42:30,600
Perhaps because of this, the Titanic has never really left the public consciousness.

482
00:42:31,780 --> 00:42:35,480
There were a lot of changes made due to the sinking of the Titanic.

483
00:42:35,640 --> 00:42:43,420
Of course, the first and most important one was that all ships must carry enough lifeboats for the number of people aboard.

484
00:42:46,060 --> 00:42:50,780
Legislation tightened the regulations that defined the use of wireless telegraphy aboard ship.

485
00:42:51,260 --> 00:42:55,040
giving increased priority to safety reports at the expense of personal messages.

486
00:42:55,660 --> 00:42:57,480
Shipping lanes were moved farther south.

487
00:42:58,400 --> 00:43:01,320
Emergency drills became mandatory on passenger ships.

488
00:43:01,920 --> 00:43:05,900
The White Star Line also made specific changes to the Titanic's sister ships.

489
00:43:07,200 --> 00:43:10,820
They pulled Olympic from service, sent her back to the yards.

490
00:43:10,821 --> 00:43:15,920
They stopped work on hole number 433, which became Britannic.

491
00:43:15,921 --> 00:43:21,700
and they incorporated these features, a couple of which, most famous,

492
00:43:22,320 --> 00:43:26,000
are the double hull, the extension of the double bottom all the way up around the hull,

493
00:43:26,740 --> 00:43:31,520
and vertical extension of key watertight bulkheads.

494
00:43:32,640 --> 00:43:37,020
There is no compelling evidence to suggest that any of these engineering solutions

495
00:43:37,021 --> 00:43:39,840
would have had a significant effect on the sinking of the Titanic.

496
00:43:42,060 --> 00:43:44,740
Sealed bulkheads may have slowed the rate of her sinking,

497
00:43:44,741 --> 00:43:46,620
but would not have prevented it

498
00:43:46,621 --> 00:43:48,800
because too many bulkheads were breached

499
00:43:48,801 --> 00:43:50,200
for the ship to retain buoyancy.

500
00:43:53,940 --> 00:43:56,760
The enduring lesson of the Titanic might be

501
00:43:56,761 --> 00:43:58,480
that the achievements of technology

502
00:43:58,481 --> 00:44:00,280
sometimes mask vulnerabilities.

503
00:44:01,860 --> 00:44:04,540
But risk can never be engineered away,

504
00:44:05,140 --> 00:44:06,420
no matter how impressive

505
00:44:06,421 --> 00:44:08,800
our technological marvels appear.

